      ":question/id": "a9fc60b2-56c5-5c77-91bc-0585b6ed8c86",
      ":question/problem": "Find the antiderivative of \n$\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}-x+\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int (\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}-x+\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})\\,\\text{d}x\\\\ &= \\int {x}^{4/5}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int {x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4/5+1}}{(\\frac{4}{5}+1)}-\\frac{{x}^{1+1}}{(1+1)}+2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{-4+1}}{(-4+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{9/5}}{\\left(\\frac{9}{5}\\right)}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{-3}}{(-3)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{9}}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
